===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[ignominious|Ignominious]]; [[disgraceful]]. {{defdate|from 1573|refn=OED}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare King John|act=V|scene=i|page=18|column=2|passage=Oh '''inglorious''' league: / Shall we vpon the footing of our land, / Send fayre-play-orders, and make comprimiſe, / Inſinuation, parley, and baſe truce / To Armes Inuaſiue?}}
#* {{RQ:Mary Shelley Frankenstein|edition=3rd|page=54|passage=Resolved to pursue no '''inglorious''' career, he turned his eyes toward the East, as affording scope for his spirit of enterprise.}}
#* {{RQ:London White Fang|chapter=The Famine|page=173|passage=[H]e cast about in his mind for a way to beat a retreat not too '''inglorious'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1945|month=September and October|author=C. Hamilton Ellis|title=Royal Trains—V|journal=Railway Magazine|page=251|text=The last occasion on which the Kaiser {{quote-gloss|{{w|Wilhelm II}}}} used this train was for an '''inglorious''' journey into Holland towards the end of the 1914 war. He spent the night in it at Eysden {{quote-gloss|{{l|en|Eijsden}}}}, while the Queen of the Netherlands and a hastily summoned Cabinet debated what to do with him.}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare}}<ref name="OED"/> Not [[famous]]; [[obscure]]. {{defdate|from 1591|refn=OED}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Regained|lines=39–42|page=56|passage=Great [[w:Julius Caesar|''Julius'' {{quote-gloss|Caesar}}]], whom now all the world admires / The more he grew in years, the more inflam’d / With glory, wept that he had liv’d ſo long / '''Inglorious''': but thou yet art not too late.}}

